"""Bounded recursive filesystem walking with explicit traversal events.

This module is a separate traversal API; `read_dir` remains a one-directory
iterator. A `Walker` owns the native traversal state and emits one owned
`WalkEvent` at a time, so callers do not need an unbounded array of paths.
Traversal is blocking hosted-filesystem I/O and must not be placed in inference,
impulse forwarding, neurogenesis, or another realtime path.
"""
import {
    io,
    path as path_module,
};

"""Selects how a walker reports an entry that cannot be traversed.

The policy applies to metadata, child-directory opening, and cycle failures.
It never converts a failed entry into a successful file or directory event.
"""
open enum WalkErrorPolicy {
    """Stop immediately and return the mapped `IoError` from the next call.

    The walker remains owned by the caller and must still be closed or allowed
    to reach its `Drop` performance after the error is observed.
    """
    FailFast,
    """Emit one `WalkEventKind.Error` event and continue with later siblings.

    The event contains the affected path and a typed error value. The walker
    does not descend through the failed entry and does not retry it implicitly.
    """
    EmitError,
}

"""Classifies one streaming event from a recursive walker.

For a directory, traversal is depth-first: an enter event precedes its child
events and a leave event follows them. A root file produces one `File` event;
a root symlink produces one `Symlink` event when links are not followed.
"""
open enum WalkEventKind {
    """The walker entered a directory and may emit its children.

    The event path identifies the directory and `depth` is its zero-based
    distance from the requested root.
    """
    EnterDirectory,
    """The event path is a regular file or followed non-directory target.

    The event is terminal for that path; no child traversal follows it.
    """
    File,
    """The event path is a symbolic link that was not followed.

    This is the default link behavior and also applies to a link encountered
    below a directory when `follow_symlinks` is false.
    """
    Symlink,
    """The walker finished one directory and returned to its parent.

    A directory that was entered emits exactly one matching leave event,
    including the root directory when traversal reaches end of stream.
    """
    LeaveDirectory,
    """The event contains a typed traversal error and was not descended into.

    This variant is produced only under `WalkErrorPolicy.EmitError`; fail-fast
    traversal returns the error from `next_walk_event` instead.
    """
    Error,
}

"""Traversal policy for one bounded recursive walker.

`max_depth` is zero-based: zero emits only the requested root directory and
its matching enter/leave events. A child is visited only when its depth is at
most `max_depth`. `follow_symlinks` is false by default in callers; when true,
canonical directory identities are tracked to reject cycles. `order` uses the
same native/lexicographic contract as directory iteration independently at
each directory level. `include_hidden` controls leading-dot names, not host
attributes. `error_policy` selects whether failures stop the call or become
events. The hosted implementation bounds depth, visited directories, entries,
and entry-name size before retaining traversal state.
"""
open struct WalkOptions {
    """Maximum emitted descendant depth; the root is depth zero.

    `0u32` is useful for inspecting only the root lifecycle. Values above the
    hosted profile limit are rejected as invalid input rather than truncated.
    """
    erg max_depth: u32,
    """Follow symbolic links only when explicitly enabled.

    Following links resolves directory identity and enables cycle detection;
    disabling it reports links as `Symlink` without reading their targets.
    """
    erg follow_symlinks: Bool,
    """Include leading-dot names when true.

    The option affects names encountered in each directory and does not alter
    metadata visibility or the treatment of a root path. The `erg` field is
    owned by the caller's `WalkOptions` value and is read immutably by `walk`.
    """
    erg include_hidden: Bool,
    """Ordering policy applied independently at every directory level.

    `Native` preserves host iterator order. `Lexicographic` uses the canonical
    platform byte/code-unit representation and is the deterministic choice.
    """
    erg order: DirectoryOrder,
    """Fail-fast or emit a typed error event for traversal failures.

    Permission, missing-target, unsupported, cycle, and generic failures are
    never silently discarded. The `erg` policy is owned by the options value;
    passing it to `walk` borrows the value and does not consume the policy.
    """
    erg error_policy: WalkErrorPolicy,
}

"""One owned event produced by `next_walk_event`.

`path` is an owned snapshot and does not borrow the walker's internal frame.
`depth` is zero-based relative to the requested root. `error` is meaningful
only for `WalkEventKind.Error`; other events carry `IoError.Failed` as a
sentinel. The event is consumed by the caller and retains no borrowed storage
after the call returns.
"""
open struct WalkEvent {
    """Owned path of the directory, file, link, or failed traversal.

    The path remains valid independently of the native directory frame and is
    released with the event according to normal Actus ownership rules.
    """
    erg path: path_module::Path,
    """Zero-based depth relative to the requested root.

    The root is always depth zero; descendants increment by one directory
    level, including entries reached through a followed symbolic link.
    """
    erg depth: u32,
    """Event classification for the owned path snapshot.

    The kind determines whether the path represents lifecycle, data, link, or
    error state and therefore whether a child event can follow it.
    """
    erg kind: WalkEventKind,
    """Typed cause when `kind` is `Error`; otherwise a sentinel value.

    Consumers must inspect `kind` before interpreting this field.
    """
    erg error: io::IoError,
}

"""Opaque owner for one streaming recursive traversal.

The handle is a runtime token, not an operating-system descriptor and not a
serialized application value. It owns retained directory frames and the
cycle-detection state until explicit close, move, or automatic Drop cleanup.
"""
open struct Walker {
    """Runtime token for bounded traversal state and retained directory frames.

    Callers pass the owner as `ins` to advance it, transfer it as `dat` to
    close it, and must not reuse it after that transfer.
    """
    erg handle: Int,
}

"""Neutral output fields written by the walker runtime bridge."""
struct WalkEventData {
    erg depth: i32,
    erg kind: i32,
    erg error: i32,
}

"""Open one bounded recursive walker without recursively changing `read_dir`.

The bridge receives a validated lexical path and scalar policy values. It
allocates only bounded native traversal state, returns a positive runtime
token on success, and maps invalid policy, unavailable paths, and allocation
or host failures to negative status codes. It does not expose host path or
directory-handle types to Actus code.
"""
unsafe extern "C" verb actus_walker_open(
    abs path: path_module::Path,
    erg max_depth: Int,
    erg follow_symlinks: Bool,
    erg include_hidden: Bool,
    erg order: Int,
    erg error_policy: Int
) -> Int;

"""Fill one owned walk event and return zero, EOF, or a traversal failure.

`path` is caller-owned output storage and `output` is a caller-owned scalar
event record. The bridge copies the next event into them; `-2` means clean end
of stream and negative failure statuses leave no successful event behind.
"""
unsafe extern "C" verb actus_walker_next(
    abs handle: Int,
    ins path: path_module::Path,
    ins output: WalkEventData
) -> Int;

"""Release the runtime traversal token and all retained directory frames.

Closing is explicit and consumes the Actus owner. A second close or an invalid
token returns `IoError.Failed`; normal Drop cleanup uses the same runtime
release operation as a best-effort fallback.
"""
unsafe extern "C" verb actus_walker_close(abs handle: Int) -> Int;

verb walk_order_code(abs order: DirectoryOrder) -> Int {
    return case order {
        DirectoryOrder.Native => 0,
        DirectoryOrder.Lexicographic => 1,
    };
}

verb walk_error_policy_code(abs policy: WalkErrorPolicy) -> Int {
    return case policy {
        WalkErrorPolicy.FailFast => 0,
        WalkErrorPolicy.EmitError => 1,
    };
}

verb walk_path_placeholder() -> Result[path_module::Path, io::IoError] {
    erg raw: Buffer = Buffer[0];
    append(raw, 46u8);
    append(raw, 0u8);
    erg result = path_module::path_from_ascii(storage: dat raw);
    return case dat result {
        Result.Err(_) => Err(io::IoError.InvalidInput),
        Result.Ok(path) => Ok(path),
    };
}

verb discard_walk_handle(dat handle: Int) -> Int {
    return handle;
}

verb discard_walk_path(dat path: path_module::Path) {}

"""Start a bounded recursive traversal from `path`.

`path` is an immutable lexical input and remains owned by the caller. `options`
is also borrowed immutably and controls depth, link handling, hidden entries,
ordering, and failure delivery. On success the returned `Walker` owns the
native traversal state; on failure no walker is returned and the input path
remains unchanged. The operation may perform blocking host metadata and
directory I/O, but it does not read file contents or publish filesystem state.
"""
open verb walk(abs path: path_module::Path, abs options: WalkOptions) -> Result[Walker, io::IoError] {
    erg depth: Int = options.max_depth as Int;
    erg follow = options.follow_symlinks;
    erg hidden = options.include_hidden;
    erg order = walk_order_code(order: abs options.order);
    erg policy = walk_error_policy_code(policy: abs options.error_policy);
    erg handle = actus_walker_open(
        path: abs path,
        max_depth: erg depth,
        follow_symlinks: erg follow,
        include_hidden: erg hidden,
        order: erg order,
        error_policy: erg policy
    );
    return case handle {
        -1 => {
            discard_walk_handle(handle: dat handle);
            Err(io::IoError.Failed);
        },
        -2 => {
            discard_walk_handle(handle: dat handle);
            Err(io::IoError.Failed);
        },
        -3 => {
            discard_walk_handle(handle: dat handle);
            Err(io::IoError.InvalidInput);
        },
        _ => Ok(Walker {
            handle: handle,
        }),
    };
}

verb walk_event_kind(abs kind: i32) -> WalkEventKind {
    return case kind {
        0 => WalkEventKind.EnterDirectory,
        1 => WalkEventKind.File,
        2 => WalkEventKind.Symlink,
        3 => WalkEventKind.LeaveDirectory,
        _ => WalkEventKind.Error,
    };
}

verb walk_event_error(abs error: i32) -> io::IoError {
    return case error {
        1 => io::IoError.Failed,
        2 => io::IoError.Failed,
        4 => io::IoError.Unsupported,
        _ => io::IoError.Failed,
    };
}

"""Return the next event from a streaming traversal.

`walker` is an exclusive call-scoped loan and is restored for the caller after
the event is produced. `Ok(Some(event))` transfers one owned event to the
caller, `Ok(None)` is clean end of stream, and `Err(error)` represents a
fail-fast traversal failure. The method never returns an event backed by a
borrowed native directory entry and does not read file payloads.
"""
open verb next_walk_event(ins walker: Walker) -> Result[Option[WalkEvent], io::IoError] {
    erg path_result = walk_path_placeholder();
    return case dat path_result {
        Result.Err(error) => Err(error),
        Result.Ok(path) => {
            erg output = WalkEventData {
                depth: 0i32,
                kind: 4i32,
                error: 0i32,
            };
            erg status = actus_walker_next(
                handle: abs walker.handle,
                path: ins path,
                output: ins output
            );
            return case status {
                -2 => {
                    discard_walk_path(path: dat path);
                    Ok(Option[WalkEvent].None);
                },
                -1 => {
                    discard_walk_path(path: dat path);
                    Err(io::IoError.Failed);
                },
                _ => Ok(Option[WalkEvent].Some(WalkEvent {
                    path: path,
                    depth: output.depth as u32,
                    kind: walk_event_kind(kind: abs output.kind),
                    error: walk_event_error(error: abs output.error),
                })),
            };
        },
    };
}

"""Close a walker explicitly and release all traversal frames.

`walker` is transferred as `dat`, so it cannot be reused after this call.
Successful close returns `Ok(0)`; an invalid or already-released runtime token
returns `Err(IoError.Failed)`. Callers may rely on `Drop` for early exits, but
explicit close is the observable success path when release status matters.
"""
open verb close_walker(dat walker: Walker) -> Result[Int, io::IoError] {
    erg status = actus_walker_close(handle: abs walker.handle);
    return case status {
        -1 => Err(io::IoError.Failed),
        _ => Ok(status),
    };
}

"""Provide best-effort automatic cleanup for abandoned walker owners.

The performance runs when ownership leaves scope without an explicit close.
It releases the native token exactly once from the Actus ownership path and
does not replace the explicit `close_walker` status-bearing operation.
"""
open perform Drop for Walker {
    """Release the owned runtime traversal token exactly once.

    `self` is an `ins` loan of the Drop owner; the performance may inspect its
    handle for cleanup but does not transfer the owner to another binding.
    """
    verb drop(ins self: Walker) {
        actus_walker_close(handle: abs self.handle);
    }
}
